 ],
 "literal": "To us indeed, turbid liquor he gives, he himself / clear liquor drinks, the king, greatly! / He is a bad man, certainly, the king, now! / Enemies' difficult fortress having besieged, / biting his lips, roaring, \"You go first!\" he did not say!",
 "translation": "To us he gives the muddy dregs of the liquor, while he himself drinks the clear, filtered portion—how very bad our king has become now! For, when we besieged the enemies' formidable fortress, biting his lips and roaring, he never said to me, \"You go first!\"",
 "modern": "எங்களுக்குக் கலங்கலான மதுவைத் தருகிறான், தான் மட்டும் தெளிந்த மதுவைக் குடிக்கிறான்; நம் அரசன் இப்போது மிகவும் கெட்டவன் ஆகிவிட்டான்! பகைவர்களின் அரிய கோட்டையை முற்றுகையிட்டு, உதட்டைக் கடித்துக் கர்ஜித்துக்கொண்டு, \"நீ முதலில் செல்!\" என்று அவன் எனக்குச் சொல்லவே இல்லை.",
 "explanation": "This poem is a beautiful example of praise in the guise of blame (vanchap pukalchi) from the Puranaanooru collection. A warrior affectionately complains about his king's behavior during peace and war. In times of feasting, the king keeps the clear, refined liquor for himself and gives the thick, muddy dregs to his soldiers. But when war comes and they besiege the enemy's strong fortress, the king, biting his lips in fury and roaring, does not tell his soldiers to go first; instead, he rushes into the danger himself. The warrior humorously calls the king 'bad' (innaan) for this, but it actually highlights the king's supreme courage and protective love for his men, as he leads from the front rather than sending his soldiers to face death first.",
